{"ts": 0.065831, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[90m──\u001b[0m \u001b[90mModule\u001b[0m \u001b[90m───────────────────────────────────────────────────────\u001b[0m \u001b[93mshells  1/3\u001b[0m \u001b[90m──\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n", "len": 247}
{"ts": 0.065937, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[1;97mThe shell is a program\u001b[0m\r\n\r\nThe shell reads your line, finds the program you named, and runs it.\r\n", "len": 107}
{"ts": 0.065963, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "It is itself a program, and you can look at it.\r\n\r\n  \u001b[92mecho $BASH_VERSION\u001b[0m       \u001b[90m# the version of the shell running now\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[92mps -p $$ -o pid,comm\u001b[0m     \u001b[90m# this shell, as a running program\u001b[0m\r\n\r\nThe second line prints one row: a number and a name. The name ends in\r\nbash. The shell is a program like any other; \u001b[92m$$\u001b[0m is its number.\r\n\r\n\u001b[93m▶ Run both, then type \u001b[92mnext\u001b[93m.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n", "len": 417}
{"ts": 0.06921, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[1;90mnext\u001b[0m \u001b[90m(go on)\u001b[0m   \u001b[1;90mshow\u001b[0m \u001b[90m(this screen again)\u001b[0m   \u001b[1;90mprev\u001b[0m \u001b[90m(back)\u001b[0m   \u001b[1;90mquit\u001b[0m \u001b[90m(save and leave)\u001b[0m\r\n", "len": 159}
