{"ts": 0.098379, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[1;97mOther shells exist\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[92mbash\u001b[0m is common on Linux and is what this tutor runs. \u001b[92mzsh\u001b[0m is the\r\ndefault on macOS. \u001b[92mfish\u001b[0m is another, installed separately.\r\n\r\n  \u001b[92mcommand -v zsh fish\u001b[0m     \u001b[90m# one path for each that is installed\u001b[0m\r\n\r\nNo line for a shell means it is not installed. That is normal. What\r\ndiffers between shells is setup and prompt; \u001b[92mls\u001b[0m, paths and pipes are\r\nthe same in all of them.\r\n\r\n\u001b[93m▶ Run it, then type \u001b[92mnext\u001b[93m.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n", "len": 495}
