{"ts": 0.126907, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "\u001b[1;97mYour login shell and this shell\u001b[0m\r\n\r\nYour \u001b[1mlogin shell\u001b[0m is the one a new terminal window starts. It can\r\n", "len": 120}
{"ts": 0.126925, "dir": "out", "enc": "utf8", "data": "differ from the shell running now.\r\n\r\n  \u001b[92mecho $SHELL\u001b[0m            \u001b[90m# your login shell\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[92mecho $BASH_VERSION\u001b[0m     \u001b[90m# the shell running now\u001b[0m\r\n\r\nThis tutor always runs its lessons in bash, whatever your login shell\r\nis. What you practise here works the same under zsh.\r\n\r\n\u001b[93m▶ Compare the two lines, then type \u001b[92mnext\u001b[93m for the task.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n", "len": 376}
